---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import ConversionCalculator from '@components/islands/ConversionCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('everyday', 'conversion-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'What units can I convert?', answer: 'Length, weight/mass, volume, area, speed, digital storage and temperature. Pick a category, choose your "from" and "to" units, enter a value and press Convert.' },
  { question: 'How many kilometres are in a mile?', answer: 'One mile is 1.609 kilometres, and one kilometre is 0.621 miles. Other everyday ones: 1 kg = 2.205 lb, 1 inch = 2.54 cm, and 1 US gallon = 3.785 litres.' },
  { question: 'How is temperature converted?', answer: 'Temperature uses formulas rather than simple multipliers. For example, Celsius to Fahrenheit is (°C × 9/5) + 32, and Celsius to Kelvin is °C + 273.15. The calculator applies the correct formula automatically.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between KB and KiB?', answer: 'A kilobyte (KB) is 1,000 bytes, while a kibibyte (KiB) is 1,024 bytes. The calculator supports both decimal (KB, MB, GB) and binary (KiB, MiB, GiB) units in the digital storage category.' },
  { question: 'Are the conversion factors exact?', answer: 'Yes — they use internationally defined values, such as one inch being exactly 2.54 cm and one mile exactly 1,609.344 m. Results are rounded for display but computed from the exact factors.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" rail intro="Convert between units of length, weight, volume, area, speed, digital storage and temperature — accurately, using internationally standard factors." faqs={faqs} reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-09' }}>
  <ConversionCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use the converter</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick a <strong>category</strong> — length, weight, volume, area, speed, digital storage or temperature.</li>
      <li>Select the unit you are converting <strong>from</strong> and the unit you are converting <strong>to</strong>.</li>
      <li>Enter a <strong>value</strong> and press <strong>Convert</strong> to read the result.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Switching category refreshes the available units automatically, so the "from" and "to"
      lists always match what you are measuring. You can swap the direction just by reversing
      your unit choices.
    </p>

    <h2>What it converts</h2>
    <p>Each category holds the units that people most often need to move between:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Length</strong> — millimetres, centimetres, metres, kilometres, inches, feet, yards and miles.</li>
      <li><strong>Weight / mass</strong> — milligrams, grams, kilograms, tonnes, ounces, pounds and stone.</li>
      <li><strong>Volume</strong> — millilitres, litres, cubic metres and US teaspoons, tablespoons, fluid ounces, cups, pints, quarts and gallons.</li>
      <li><strong>Area</strong> — square centimetres, square metres, hectares, square kilometres, square feet, acres and square miles.</li>
      <li><strong>Speed</strong> — metres per second, km/h, mph, knots and feet per second.</li>
      <li><strong>Digital storage</strong> — bytes through terabytes, in both decimal (KB, MB, GB) and binary (KiB, MiB, GiB) units.</li>
      <li><strong>Temperature</strong> — Celsius, Fahrenheit and Kelvin.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How factor-based conversion works</h2>
    <p>
      Every unit in a category stores a factor to that category's base unit — metres for
      length, kilograms for mass, litres for volume, square metres for area, metres per second
      for speed and bytes for storage. To convert, the tool multiplies your value by the "from"
      factor to reach the base unit, then divides by the "to" factor to reach the target unit.
    </p>
    <p>
      That two-step design keeps every pair consistent: because one mile is defined as exactly
      1,609.344 metres and one kilometre as 1,000, a mile is always precisely 1.609344
      kilometres, with no rounding drift between them.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Convert <strong>5 miles</strong> to kilometres. Miles carry a factor of 1,609.344 metres
      and kilometres a factor of 1,000, so the result is 5 × (1,609.344 ÷ 1,000) = 5 × 1.609344
      = <strong>8.047 kilometres</strong>. Reverse it and 100 km is 100 × (1,000 ÷ 1,609.344) ≈
      <strong>62.14 miles</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Common conversions at a glance</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Multiply by</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Miles</td><td>Kilometres</td><td>1.609</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kilograms</td><td>Pounds</td><td>2.205</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inches</td><td>Centimetres</td><td>2.54</td></tr>
        <tr><td>US gallons</td><td>Litres</td><td>3.785</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Celsius</td><td>Fahrenheit</td><td>× 9/5, then + 32</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Why temperature is different</h2>
    <p>
      Temperature scales do not share a zero point, so they use formulas rather than a single
      multiplier. The converter routes every temperature through Celsius and applies the
      standard relationships:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Conversion</th><th>Formula</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Celsius → Fahrenheit</td><td>(°C × 9/5) + 32</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fahrenheit → Celsius</td><td>(°F − 32) × 5/9</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Celsius → Kelvin</td><td>°C + 273.15</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>So 20°C is (20 × 9/5) + 32 = <strong>68°F</strong>, and also 20 + 273.15 = <strong>293.15 K</strong>.</p>

    <h2>Decimal vs binary storage</h2>
    <p>
      Digital storage has two conventions, and the calculator supports both. Decimal units step
      by 1,000 — a kilobyte is 1,000 bytes — while binary units step by 1,024, so a kibibyte
      (KiB) is 1,024 bytes and a mebibyte (MiB) is 1,048,576. Drive makers quote decimal
      gigabytes while operating systems often report binary ones, which is why a "1 TB" disk can
      look slightly smaller once installed.
    </p>
    <p>
      All factors are the internationally defined values, so results are exact and only rounded
      for display. For currency-style financial conversions, see our
      <a href="/finance">financial calculators</a>; to turn a floor measurement into an area,
      try the <a href="/everyday/square-footage-calculator">square footage calculator</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
